One pot synthesis of $\mathbf{a}$ –amylase metal organic framework (MOF)–sponge via dip-coating technique
Shamraja S. Nadar, Virendra K. Rathod
Department of Chemical Engineering, Institute of Chemical Technology, Matunga (E) Mumbai-400019, India. Corresponding Author: Dr. Virendra K. Rathod E-mail: vk.rathod@ictmumbai.edu.in, Phone: +91-22-33612020, Fax: 91-22-33611020.
Abstract
Enzyme embedded metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are blooming in the field of enzyme immobilisation on account of their superior catalytic efficiency, thermal and chemical stability as compared to native enzyme. However, the separation and recovery of enzyme embedded MOF composite is quite challenging due to its nanometer size, high dispersity and low density which limits the reusability. In this work, α–amylase embedded ZIF-67 was fabricated onto melamine sponge using surfactant assisted dip coating technique. This was achieved in single pot making the method facile and rapid. α–Amylase MOF–sponge was analysed and characterized by FT–IR, XRD, SEM and TGA. We demonstrated that the encapsulation could maintain the active conformational structure of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase after immobilization procedure with excellent bioactivity. Further, apart from merits of framework shielding, it offers mass transfer limitation for macromolecular substrate (like starch) which was determined and expressed as effectiveness factor (η) by using Michaelis–Menten enzyme kinetics. At the end, recycling studies were carried out for $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge which showed $32\%$ residual activity after six consecutive cycles. It is believed that this strategy has great potential in the field of continuous biotransformation (catalysis), biosensing and biomedicine application.
Key words: α–Amylase; melamine sponge; immobilization
## Introduction
$\mathfrak{a}$ –Amylase, a starch hydrolysing enzyme which has commercial application in numerous sectors including food, pharmaceutical, textile, paper, detergent, and other industries [1,2]. Although enzymes can catalyse reactions with great specificity under the benign environmental conditions, the catalytic performance of native enzymes is considerably hampered by the surrounding environment, in particular, the harsher and fluctuating bioprocessing conditions [3–5]. Also, separation of enzyme in active conformation from reaction mixture is not only difficult but also costly. Enzyme immobilization has been envisioned as one of the most efficient methodologies to enhance the catalytic properties (such as pH and thermo-stability), easy separation and recyclability for multiple cycles [6–8]. The nanomaterials could be the potential support for enzyme immobilization [9]. Various nanomaterials such as magnetic nanoparticles, graphene oxide nanocomposite and magnetic silica composites had been used as a support for enzyme immobilization [10–12].
From last decade, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) a novel class of porous crystalline inorganic–organic hybrid materials, have been exclusively studied due to their fascinating physico-chemical properties such as high surface areas, large pore volume, structural tenability, crystallographic structure and designable organic ligands. MOF materials have become interesting material for applications in various sectors such as gas separation, catalysis, sensor, drug delivery and electronics [13–16]. In recent years, MOFs are designed to immobilize enzyme which not only improves the enzyme stability but also maintain their active conformation, with minimal leaching. It also helps to enhance thermal and operational stability along with long-term storage stability of enzyme [17–19]. They are synthesized by three different routes: (i) surface adsorption, (ii) covalent binding and (iii) cage inclusion [20–22]. Various enzymes such as horseradish peroxidase (HRP) [23], catalase [24], glucose oxidase (GOx) [25], glucoamylase [26], trypsin [27], lipase [28] and few others have been immobilized in the form of enzyme-MOF composite. Though, these composites exhibited superior catalytic properties, the recovery and separation of enzyme-MOF composite from the reaction mixture is arduous due to its nanometer size and high dispersity. Further, enzyme-MOF composites have tendency to aggregate which limit their accessibility in the reaction, thereby causing mass transfer limitation [22,29,30].
In order to overcome these hurdles, significant efforts have been made to fabricate MOF materials onto three dimensional (3D) architectures as a support matrix [31]. Herein, we used commercially available melamine sponge as a 3D matrix which is economic, light-weight, and porous for coating $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ –amylase embedded MOF on it [32]. The high nitrogen content of melamine sponge provides abundant binding sites for enzyme-MOF, which makes it a great support [33]. In addition, spatial structure of porous sponge provides an excellent permeability and good mechanical strength. α–Amylase MOF–sponge was prepared by encapsulating $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ –amylase within ZIF-67 followed by loading on melamine sponge via simple surfactant-assisted dip-coating method at ambient temperature. The prepared $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge was analysed and characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR), thermo gravimetric analysis (TGA) and confocal scanning laser microscopy. The morphological (size and shape) studies were done by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). In addition, the diffusion effect caused due framework around enzyme molecules was expressed as an effectiveness factor. After immobilization process, structural conformation of α–amylase was quantified (in terms of $\upbeta$ -sheet, random coil, $\mathfrak{a}$ –helix and $\upbeta$ - turn structure) by Fourier transform infrared analysis data tools. At the end, industrial feasibility was determined by carrying out reusability studies.
## Materials and methods
## Materials
Cobalt nitrate, 2-methylimmidazole, α–Amylase (29 U/mg, originated from Bacillus amyloliquefaciens) and sodium dodecyl benzene sulfonate were purchased from Sigma, St. Louis, USA. Commercial melamine sponge was procured from Matrix Poly Pac, Chennai, India. Potato starch and 3,5-dinitrosalicylic acid (DNS) were bought from from HiMedia Laboratories Pvt. Ltd., Mumbai, India. All the other reagents were purchased from Sigma Aldrich and used without further purification.
## $\mathbf{a}$ –Amylase MOF–sponge synthesis
The enzyme embedded MOF–sponge was synthesized by dip coating melamine sponge in solution containing pre-synthesised enzyme entrapped ZIF-67. Briefly, aqueous solution of cobalt nitrate (237 mg, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was mixed with containing 2-methylimidazole solution (272 mg, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) containing enzyme ( $\mathrm{1~mg/mL)}$ . Then, the mixture was kept under stirring (200 rpm) at room temperature (RT, $28{\pm}2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30 min. The purple coloured precipitate product ( $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF) was obtained. Further, pre-washed melamine sponge ( $2\times2\times2$ cm) was coated with sodium dodecyl benzene sulfonate $(4\mathrm{mg/mL})$ and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This, surfactant coated melamine sponge was dipped in the pre-synthesized $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF and stirred for 1 h at RT. The obtained enzyme immobilized sponge was washed with DI water and further with buffer $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ and kept for overnight drying. The obtained purple coloured sponge was referred as $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge.
The pure ZIF-67 crystal was synthesized using the above method but without enzyme for comparative and characterization purpose in the subsequent studies.
## $\mathbf{a}$ –Amylase activity and protein assay
Enzyme activity was determined by hydrolysing potato starch under optimized condition. Briefly, the potato starch $(1\%\ \mathrm{w/v)}$ was used as substrate and mixed with appropriate amount of enzyme. The reaction mixture was incubated at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . After hydrolysis, the amount of released reducing sugar was estimated by using DNS at $540~\mathrm{nm}$ spectroscopically (Jasco V-630, Japan) [34]. One unit (U) activity is defined as the amount of enzyme required to release one micro-mole product (maltose equivalent) per unit time at optimum condition. The immobilization yield was determined by measuring protein concentration with the help of Bradford protein assay. Briefly, the supernatant was added into Braford’s reagent (5 mL) and incubated for 5 min. The absorption was measured by UV-Vis spectrophotometer (Jasco V-630, Japan) at $595~\mathrm{nm}$ [35]. The experiments were done in triplicate and the error bar represents the percentage error in each set of readings.
## Characterization of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge
The prepared $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge was characterized using Fourier transform infrared (FT–IR, Bruker VERTEX 80-v vacuum FT–IR spectrometers, USA) spectroscopy by scanning full spectra $(400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of pure ZIF67 and enzyme embedded MOF were recorded by XRD (Philips PW 1830 X-ray Diffraction, Japan) to investigate crystal structures. The morphology of $\mathfrak{a}$ –Amylase MOF–sponge was done by putter-coating the sample with conductive thin layer of gold and images were captured by scanning electron microscope (SEM, JEOL - JSM-6380, Japan) under $15.0\mathrm{kV}$ .
## Mass transfer limitation studies
The extent of mass transfer was determined for free $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase, $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF and $\scriptstyle{{\mathfrak{a}}-{}}$ amylase MOF–sponge by Michaelis–Menten enzyme kinetics and expressed in terms of effectiveness factor [36]. Effectiveness factor is defined as the ratio of activity of immobilized enzyme to free form of enzyme. It was calculated for varying starch concentration from $50{\mathrm{-}}750\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Effectiveness factor was determined as
Effectiveness factor $\mathrm{(\upeta)=\frac{V_{\mathrm{immobilized\alpha\alpha-Amylase}}}{V_{f r e e\alpha-A m y l a s e}}}$ where, Vfree $\mathfrak{a}$ –Amylase and Vimmobilized $\mathfrak{a}$ –Amylase are the initial rates of reaction of free and immobilized enzyme, respectively.
## Secondary structural analysis
FT–IR analytical tools were used to estimate changes in fractions of secondary structure after immobilization of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase according to the reported literature [37,38]. The FT–IR spectra (from 1200 to $1800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) of native and immobilized form of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase were recorded using Shimadzu IRAffinity-1 FT–IR spectrophotometer. The obtained FT–IR spectra of amide I region $(1600{-}1700~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ) was derivatized into second order by using Essential FTIR™ ver. 3 and further smoothened with a 13-point Savitzky-Golay. Further, the area under amide I bands curves was quantified using multi-peak fitting Gaussian function in Origin 9.0.
## Recycling and reusing of $\mathbf{a}$ –amylase MOF-sponge
Reusability of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge was evaluated by using soluble starch as a substrate. The starch hydrolytic reaction was carried out at optimum physiological conditions in batch mode. After each cycle, enzyme immobilized sponge was separated and washed with DI, then used in fresh substrate solution for next cycle. α–Amylase MOF–sponge was tested upto six successive cycles considering residual activity of first cycle as $100\%$ .
## Results and discussion
## Preparation of α–amylase MOF–sponge
In a typical immobilization experiment, aqueous solution of cobalt nitrate and enzyme solution containing 2-methylimidazole was added drop wise and stirred at RT. The colourless solution immediately turned into purple coloured solution after addition of cobalt nitrate (Figure S1a). After half an hour stirring, the obtained solution was centrifuged and washed with DI water for 4 times to remove unreacted free enzyme molecules. The enzyme was entrapped within ZIF-67 by the self-assembling of metal ions and organic ligands via coordination bonds. The prepared $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF (Figure S1b-c) showed $98.9\%$ immobilization yield which was determined by Bradford protein assay. The obtained $\scriptstyle{{\mathfrak{a}}-{}}$ amylase MOF exhibited activity of $16.25~\mathrm{U/mg}$ (relative activity of $56\%$ as compared to free enzyme). Further, the obtained $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase ZIF-67 (purple coloured powder) was dispersed in DI water. The cubes of surfactant coated sponge ( $2{\times}2{\times}2$ cm) were incubated for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ with above $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase ZIF-67 solution. The $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase ZIF-67 formed layer on the sponge skeletons which could be due to electrostatic and $\pi^{-}\pi$ stacking interaction [32]. The purple coloured solution (because of presence of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase ZIF-67) turned into colourless solution and sponge cubes were coated with $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase ZIF-67 (Figure S1c). To understand the binding mechanism, the charge on sodium dodecyl benzene sulfonate coated melamine sponge and $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ –amylase ZIF-67 was determined by using Zeta potential (Figure S2-3). It was observed that surfactant coated sponge exhibited high negative charge $\mathrm{(-28.2\mV)}$ ), whereas enzyme embedded MOF showed high positive charge $(22.2~\mathrm{mV})$ . This charge difference helps to bind ZIF-67 onto melamine sponge via electrostatic interactions [39]. Further, the prepared MOF–sponge was dried at room temperature overnight as referred further as ${\mathfrak{a}}-$ amylase MOF–sponge.
## Characterization of $\mathbf{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge
The $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ –amylase MOF–sponge was characterized and analysed by FT–IR and PXRD. Further, the morphology of α–amylase MOF–sponge was observed by SEM, while the amount of immobilized enzyme was determined by TGA.
Firstly, the melamine sponge coated with surfactant analysed by using FT–IR. In the FT–IR spectra of native melamine sponge (Figure 1a), the characteristic peaks were observed at 813.5, 1548.8, 2930 and $3367~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is correspond to the triazine ring bending, $\mathrm{C=N}$ stretching in triazine ring, C-H stretching, and N-H bending from primary and secondary amine groups, respectively [40]. After coating the sponge with sodium dodecyl benzene sulfonate (SDBS, Figure 1b), the strong peaks at 1189, 1121 and $616~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were observed due to vibration of phenyl groups [39]. This FT–IR spectra confirms the successful coating of ${\mathfrak{a}}-$ amylase MOF on melamine sponge. Further, $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF was coated onto surfactant– melamine sponge using dip-coating method. Here, the $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge (Figure 1c and S4-5) showed the $\mathrm{Co-N}$ stretching band at around $424~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ which was primarily derived from the 2-methylimmidazole ligand. The stretching mode of C–N bonding in 2- methylimmidazole was observed at peak frequency of $1584~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Additionally, the aliphatic and aromatic stretching in C–H was attributed to peaks at 2927.43 and $3133.14~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The characteristic band of 1662.02 cm−1 was seen in the spectra which represents amide band I corresponding to $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase (Figure 1d and S4) [41–43]. These results confirmed that enzymes were successfully immobilized within MOF–sponge.
The crystalline nature of prepared $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF before and after dip coating onto melamine sponge was determined by PXRD patterns. Figure 1e-g shows the PXRD pattern of plain ZIF-67 (without enzyme), α–amylase –MOF and $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge, respectively. The XRD spectra of plain ZIF-67 showed prominent reflections at $2\uptheta=7.4^{\circ}$ , $10.4^{\circ}$ , $12.7^{\circ}$ , $14.8^{\circ}$ , 16.5°, 18.0°, 22.1°, $24.5^{\circ}$ , $25.5^{\circ}$ , $26.7^{\circ}$ , $29.5^{\circ}$ , $30.6^{\circ}$ , $31.6^{\circ}$ and $32.5^{\circ}$ which correspond to (0 1 1), (0 0 2), (1 1 2) , (0 2 2), (0 1 3) , (2 2 2) , (1 1 4), (2 3 3), (2 2 4), (1 3 4), (0 4 4), (3 3 4), (2 4 4) and (2 3 5) planes respectively. These are similar to simulated patterns of pure ZIF-67 SOD-type crystal structures (Figure S6) [44]. Further, the PXRD pattern of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF with and without dip coating were similar to those of plain ZIF67. These results demonstrate that the presence of enzyme molecules and coating onto melamine sponge did not affect the intrinsic chemical/physical characteristics of ZIF-67.
Furthermore, quantitative analysis of entrapped enzyme (protein) within MOF was determined by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure S7). In TGA profile, the decomposition of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF was observed in two stage. In first-stage decomposition, a gradual weight loss in $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF was observed $(\sim23.5\%)$ till $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which can be attributed to evaporation and removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (as a major constitute) and other organic small molecules (guest molecules) from the cavities. In the $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ decomposition stage $(250–350^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , further, $9\%$ of weight loss occurred which can be attributed to the decomposition of protein/enzyme molecules within ZIF-67 [45,46]. Further, the presence of enzyme within ZIF-67 was verified by using confocal scanning laser microscopy. The fluorescently (FITC) labelled $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase was observed under confocal microscope which showed green fluorescent tagged enzyme molecules encapsulated into MOF crystals (Figure S8).
Furthermore, the morphology and size of prepared α–amylase MOF were determined by SEM. In the melamine sponge (without α–amylase MOF, Figure 1h-j), the surface and skeleton of sponge was clean and smooth. Once the sponge was modified with surfactants and loaded with $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF (Figure 1k-m), the surface of the skeleton became rough and covered with rhombic dodecahedral shaped enzyme entrapped MOF with no observable aggregation. This suggests that immobilized enzyme MOF were successfully coated on the melamine sponge via certain ionic interaction between surfactant coated melamine sponge and $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF [39]. Under a higher magnification, the size of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF was found to be in the range of 550-650 nm. These all characterization outcomes confirmed the successful preparation of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge.
## Michaelis–Menten kinetics
Michaelis–Menten parameters ( $\mathrm{\mathrm{K}}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{\mathrm{max}}}^{\mathrm{~~}}$ ) of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge and free $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase were evaluated by determining initial rates reaction with corresponding to different concentration of substrate. The $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\DeltaV_{max}}}$ values for free and immobilized enzyme is shown in Table 2. It was observed that value of $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ was increased after immobilization of ${\mathfrak{a}}-$ amylase within MOF. This indicated that the enzyme affinity towards starch decreased which could be probably due to the encapsulation of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase in the interior of frameworks restricting accessibility [47].
Further, $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge exhibited lower the rate of reaction $\mathrm{(V_{max})}$ than that of free enzyme. This might be due to conformational the changes while immobilization of enzyme [48]. Additionally, the mass transfer limitation might lead to lower the rate as compared to free form which is discussed in next section.
## Determination of mass transfer limitation
In most of entrapment methodologies, the encapsulation of enzyme molecules suffers from diffusion limitation for substrates particularly for macro-molecular substrate like starch [49]. This mass transfer limitation majorly affects the activity of immobilized enzyme can be determined in terms of effective factor (η) by using Michaelis–Menten enzyme kinetics, which is mentioned in Table 1. The η value for $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge and $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF were found to be nearly same. It shows that the loading of enzyme entrapped ZIF-67 on 3D matrix of melamine sponge did not affect the mass transfer of substrate towards the active sites of encapsulated enzyme. On the other hand, immobilized $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ –amylase results in significantly lower effectiveness factor $(\%\ n)$ than the free form. This result could be attributed to the frameworks around enzyme molecules which offer mass transfer barrier for macromolecular substrate [26].
## Secondary structure analysis
The change in the enzyme activity is directly related to the secondary structure of enzyme. After enzyme immobilization, the enzyme molecules undergo considerable changes in molecular geometry and hydrogen bonding pattern which is reflected in amide I band [50]. The amide I band $(1700{-}1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ is the most sensitive spectral region for protein’s secondary structural analysis. The spectral data obtained using FT–IR spectroscopy data tool is quite complicated and overlapping. In order to get high resolution and segregated spectra, the secondary derivative of $1700{-}1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was considered and area under curve was evaluated by multi-component peaks using Origin 9.0 [51].
The second derivative of FT–IR spectra for native enzyme, $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF and $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge were obtained (Figure S10-12).The relative contents of $\upbeta$ -sheet structure (1610- $1640~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , area under red curve), random coil structure $(1640–1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , area under green curve), $\mathfrak{a}$ –helix structure $(1650-1658~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , area under navy blue curve) and $\upbeta$ -turn structure $(1660–1700~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , area under sky blue curve) based on the modelled multi-component peak area were calculated by the software (Figure 2a-c). The change in the secondary structural fractions for free $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase, $\mathbf{a}$ –amylase MOF and $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge is summarized in Table 3. It was seen that the structural component of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase was changed during the immobilization of $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ –amylase. After entrapment of amylase, the 3D structure of enzyme remained same even after the loading onto melamine sponge. The framework around the enzyme provides significant protection to enzyme molecules [52].
## Recycling and reusing of $\mathbf{a}$ –amylase MOF-sponge
To make this immobilization technique more economical, it is necessary to study the recyclability of immobilized $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{a}}$ –amylase. In recycling study, the soluble starch was used as the substrate. After one use, enzyme immobilized sponge was separated, washed (with buffer) and recycled for next use. The residual activity of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge is depicted in Figure 3a. After 6 cycles of reuse, the immobilized enzyme exhibited $32\%$ residual activity with respect to the first cycle (considering it as a $100\%$ ). To determine the reason behind the loss of enzyme activity, the SEM image was taken after six cycles. From Figure 3b, it was seen that the amount of bounded $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF reduced after six cycles. These results indicate that the interaction between $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF and sponge was not sufficient to hold immobilized enzyme onto sponge for multiple cycles [39,53]. The recyclability of prepared biocatalyst can be improved by incorporating chemical binding (covalent binding) between $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF and functionalized melamine sponge. In this approach, the prepared ${\mathfrak{a}}-$ amylase MOF can be functionalized by carboxyl group via post synthetic modification which can be covalently bind to the amid group of melamine sponge. This approach might improve the recyclability of $\mathfrak{a}$ –amylase MOF–sponge and extend their application in wide spectra.
## Conclusion
$\mathfrak{a}$ –Amylase MOF sponge was successfully prepared by facile one pot methodology. This immobilization technique is not only rapid but also makes easy recovery of enzyme. Additionally, the commercial melamine sponge incorporated in these studies did not limit mass transfer of substrate even after the coating of α–amylase MOF on the sponge. It holds a great potential as a 3D support matrix for enzyme immobilization. Also, the prepared ${\mathfrak{a}}-$ amylase MOF–sponge was evaluated for reusability upto six cycles for its industrial feasibility. In the nutshell, the approach demonstrated here may be extended to design and easily fabricate immobilized enzyme on sponge to be used as filters and membranes for continuous operation on industrial scale. This novel approach can be implemented in the saccharification processes and to produce bioethanol in continuous mode.
